03:42.920 --> 03:45.680
the reality is a lot of times when someone's loading a page,

03:45.680 --> 03:48.200
that's probably when you're actually going to be wanting to fetch that data,

03:48.320 --> 03:49.560
and more importantly, you want to fetch it

03:49.560 --> 03:51.360
at a specific time frame.

03:51.360 --> 03:53.480
And so this is where the concept of lifecycle hooks

03:53.480 --> 03:56.480
comes into play when we're talking about front-end frameworks,

03:56.480 --> 03:58.360
and I think most of them do have them.

03:58.360 --> 04:01.480
So let's take a look at the diagram for lifecycle hooks for view,

04:01.480 --> 04:03.360
and we're going to take a look at the one directly on the docs

04:03.360 --> 04:06.440
because I want you all to be able to kind of get familiar with the docs

04:06.440 --> 04:08.680
because view has an entire team dedicated

04:08.680 --> 04:11.600
to trying to make sure that the docs are as top-notch as they can,

04:11.600 --> 04:14.080
and that basically this information is free and available to you

04:14.080 --> 04:16.760
whenever it's time for you to dive into these problems.

04:17.760 --> 04:19.120
And so if we take a look at here,

04:19.120 --> 04:22.360
here we can see an actual diagram of how the lifecycle works,

04:22.360 --> 04:25.840
and so you can see that view, what it starts to do is the renderer kicks it off,

04:25.840 --> 04:27.960
and then it runs a setup lifecycle hook,

04:27.960 --> 04:29.440
which again, we'll talk more about later.

04:29.440 --> 04:31.680
But you can see that what it's doing is it's going through

04:31.680 --> 04:35.600
and at each of these steps where you see the rounded, outlined rectangle,

04:35.600 --> 04:39.560
so we have setup, before create, created, before mount,

04:39.560 --> 04:43.720
all of these are different points inside of the lifecycle of a component,

04:43.720 --> 04:48.360
and what that means is you can actually basically interject different code

04:48.360 --> 04:49.960
depending on where you need it to run.

04:49.960 --> 04:51.640
So for example, you see here that what is it doing?

04:51.640 --> 04:54.280
First, it starts to encounter the component,

04:54.280 --> 04:56.840
it starts to initialize the options API,

04:56.840 --> 04:58.920
and then it starts to do some checks basically, right?

04:58.920 --> 05:00.800
And then it starts to render the component,

05:00.800 --> 05:03.760
it'll actually mount the thing to the actual window.

05:03.760 --> 05:06.320
Basically, you're starting to actually see the thing,

05:06.320 --> 05:07.760
and then finally, if you're deleting something,

05:07.760 --> 05:10.080
it will unmount the thing from the DOM.

05:10.080 --> 05:12.520
And so to be clear, I do not expect any of you

05:12.520 --> 05:16.720
to understand the full effect of what's happening here,

05:16.720 --> 05:20.040
but suffice to know that you can refer to this at any point in the future

05:20.040 --> 05:24.400
when you're starting to get a little bit more nuanced about where you want your code to run.

05:24.400 --> 05:27.680
For the purposes of our exercise, though, we have to think about it like this.

05:27.680 --> 05:32.440
If we wait for the component to mount before we actually start running the code to fetch,

05:32.440 --> 05:35.000
we know that asynchronous calls can take a while,

05:35.000 --> 05:37.480
and what we want to do is try to improve our user experience

05:37.480 --> 05:40.640
by ensuring that users get that data as quickly as possible,

05:40.640 --> 05:41.560
which means what?

05:41.560 --> 05:45.560
We want to actually try to initiate that fetch before the component even renders to the screen.

05:45.560 --> 05:47.560
That way, you call it, we try to time it,

05:47.560 --> 05:49.240
so that by the time the component hits the screen,

05:49.240 --> 05:52.880
you're hoping that the async data basically has hopefully finished by then.

05:52.880 --> 05:54.440
And so if we take a look at where that is,

05:54.440 --> 05:56.440
basically the way to think of it in your head at this point

05:56.440 --> 05:59.480
is mounted is the point when the mount to lifecycle hook

05:59.480 --> 06:01.560
is when it's basically showing up on the screen.

06:01.560 --> 06:03.680
That's the way to think about it.

06:03.680 --> 06:07.560
And so in other words, you have the ability to say before mount,

06:07.560 --> 06:08.520
there's one right here,

06:08.520 --> 06:11.800
but then there's also a couple of steps happening before before mount,

06:11.800 --> 06:16.520
so actually we even have the created lifecycle hook and the before create.

06:16.520 --> 06:18.880
But there's a key thing that you'll notice here though,

06:18.880 --> 06:22.720
is that with before create, this is happening before the options API.

