08:58.680 --> 09:03.960
Would it pause continuation until it returned?

09:03.960 --> 09:06.440
So your question here is around whether the lifecycle hooks

09:06.440 --> 09:06.920
are aware.

09:06.920 --> 09:09.400
Oh, can you actually just return a promise instead?

09:09.400 --> 09:13.000
Would the created not continue until the promise resolves,

09:13.000 --> 09:16.360
if you wanted to, for some reason, not mount,

09:16.360 --> 09:18.760
but until the promise is fully resolved?

09:18.760 --> 09:21.040
Before you actually move on to the next hook, basically?

09:21.040 --> 09:21.560
Yeah.

09:21.560 --> 09:25.000
So in this regard, you don't want to run it.

09:25.000 --> 09:28.160
I don't see why that wouldn't be possible.

09:28.160 --> 09:30.360
View usually has ways for you to break those things apart

09:30.360 --> 09:32.960
so that you can basically stop it.

09:32.960 --> 09:34.840
And so this would be, to be clear,

09:34.840 --> 09:36.760
this is a little bit beyond the scope of the intro,

09:36.760 --> 09:39.280
so if this goes a little beyond your current knowledge,

09:39.280 --> 09:39.880
don't worry.

09:39.880 --> 09:42.600
But basically, if you've heard of things like next tick

09:42.600 --> 09:44.040
inside of code bases, that's where

09:44.040 --> 09:47.840
people are manually initiating different stages

09:47.840 --> 09:50.040
to ensure that either different frames are being skipped

09:50.040 --> 09:51.520
or moved on.

09:51.520 --> 09:53.640
And so just know that if you're trying to intentionally

09:53.640 --> 09:55.960
stop it at the likes of the code, you'd probably

09:55.960 --> 09:58.200
have to dig into it just to figure out how that will work.

09:58.200 --> 10:00.800
Because normally, as you can see, it tries to optimize it.

10:00.800 --> 10:03.280
Because normally, you might even expect, at this point,

10:03.280 --> 10:04.880
let me go back to the code.

10:04.880 --> 10:06.840
Let me close that.

