10:33.560 --> 10:35.920
You'll see already that view is yelling at me.

10:35.920 --> 10:39.920
Because if you actually manually define it in a way keyword

10:39.920 --> 10:41.800
inside of a lifecycle hook, it will ask

10:41.800 --> 10:45.520
that you actually define the async on that directly.

10:45.520 --> 10:47.160
However, since we're not doing that,

10:47.160 --> 10:49.480
since we actually have the async already defined on the function,

10:49.480 --> 10:52.880
this is why this code still works.

10:52.880 --> 10:54.720
But otherwise, to your question, yeah,

10:54.720 --> 10:58.480
I would just dive further in into how to get it to pause.

10:58.480 --> 11:00.720
But otherwise, you should be able to do what you want.

11:00.720 --> 11:03.200
So when it comes to lifecycle hooks, basically, I would say

11:03.200 --> 11:05.360
the ones to really know about are created.

11:05.360 --> 11:06.440
Because that's usually where you're

11:06.440 --> 11:08.160
going to be running your async functions.

11:08.160 --> 11:09.240
And then if you really have something

11:09.240 --> 11:11.600
that you really want to happen right when the component is

11:11.600 --> 11:14.720
mounted, then I've seen a lot of before mount or mounted

11:14.720 --> 11:17.240
used for those scenarios, where you might want to time

11:17.240 --> 11:19.640
either some sort of animation or that kind of thing.

11:19.640 --> 11:21.080
But otherwise, just know that created

11:21.080 --> 11:23.480
is going to be your bread and butter when using the options

11:23.480 --> 11:26.480
API for doing asynchronous calls.

